# pf Provider

The pf provider manages [OpenBSD pf](https://www.openbsd.org/faq/pf/) through
[pfapid](https://github.com/pderuiter/pfapid), a privilege-separated REST API that runs on the
firewall. It manages:

* **anchors** (`pf_anchor`): rule sets loaded as `api/<name>`, typed rules only (no raw pf.conf);
* **tables** (`pf_table`): the contents of tables the firewall's base ruleset declares.

The base ruleset (`/etc/pf.conf`), the management interface and anything outside `api/*` stay out of reach.

## How changes are applied

Every create, update and delete is **commit-confirm**: pfapid loads the change, the provider confirms it over
the same connection, and pfapid rolls it back by itself if the confirmation never arrives (for example because
the new rules cut the provider off). `terraform plan` sends new rules to the firewall's `pfctl -n` and shows the
result in `pf_rules`, so syntax pf would reject fails at plan time.

pfapid accepts one pending change at a time. The provider queues its own changes and waits (up to
`change_wait`) while another client's change is pending.

Rules loaded in pf that differ from the committed ones (someone used `pfctl` by hand) are reported as a warning
on refresh and restored by the next apply.

## pfapid versions

Rule attributes `negate` and `self` (on `from`/`to`) and `icmp_types` need pfapid v0.3.0 or later; an older
pfapid refuses them at plan time. Referencing a table the base ruleset owns (for example a `const` table) needs
that table in pfapid's `policy.ref_tables`.

## Authentication

pfapid uses mutual TLS 1.3. The firewall's pfapid configuration lists its clients, each limited to certain
anchors and tables. A client is identified by its certificate's CN, or by a SPIFFE ID (`spiffe://...` URI SAN,
as in an X509-SVID), and must be issued by the CA bundle that client is bound to.

The certificates can come from anywhere:
* pfapid's built-in CA (`pfapid pki issue -name <client> -out <prefix>` on the firewall);
* your company CA, or an intermediate pfapid signs with;
* short-lived certificates from Vault PKI, SPIRE (`spiffe-helper`), step-ca or cert-manager.

`client_cert` may include intermediates after the leaf. The arguments fall back to environment variables; each
certificate argument takes PEM text or a file path. A path is read when the provider is configured, so a file that
an agent renews is picked up by the next Terraform run.

| Argument      | Environment variable  |
|---------------|-----------------------|
| `endpoint`    | `PFAPID_ENDPOINT`     |
| `ca_cert`     | `PFAPID_CA_CERT`      |
| `client_cert` | `PFAPID_CLIENT_CERT`  |
| `client_key`  | `PFAPID_CLIENT_KEY`   |
| `timeout`     | `PFAPID_TIMEOUT`      |
| `change_wait` | `PFAPID_CHANGE_WAIT`  |

